  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow... The main area of WAA today has been \nfocused more across southern Minnesota today. The lack of WAA \nand continued cloud cover and ample moisture has allowed for \ncool temperatures to continue across western and central Minnesota.\nThis has even had areas of fog hold on into the afternoon \nhours. As the trough continues to develop to our west, 850mb \nflow will become southerly across more of Minnesota and \nWisconsin. This will increase our WAA and we will also see \nadditional moisture advect in. With this additional moisture \ncloud cover will likely persist which will limit how warm we can\nget. Should still be able to get into the 60s with the chance \nfor some lower 70s still possible. This WAA should allow for \nthe motion of the broader air mass in such a way this it allows\nenough Fgen for a warm front. This front will be a factor in \nthunderstorm development tomorrow. ML CAPE looks to build during\nthe day tomorrow reaching up to around 1000 J/kg in the \nafternoon/evening based on current guidance. This combined with \nfavorable vertical shear profiles has increased chances enough \nthat SPC has added in a marginal risk for tomorrow. As for \nsevere modes, all are possible. The low level shear looks great,\nbut storms could end up mostly elevated and it would be hard \nfor them to take advantage of this. If we can get a surface \nbased storm a tornado could occur. If the storms stay elevated \nthis will stay more of hail risk. Overall this seems fairly \nconditional and storms could be quite pulsey. In the end it will\nall come down to how much surface heating we can get. If we \nunder perform on temperature it will be unlikely for us to see \nany longer lived surface based storms. The other airmass will be\nthe cooler air that starts to travel east later in the day \ntomorrow. This will create a cold front and will be another \nforcing mechanism for us tomorrow. \n\nThursday and Friday... Thursday we will behind the cold frontal\npassage and surface high pressure will move in. The airmass \nwill be cooler, but this higher pressure will finally provide \nthe setup for a return of the sun. Temperatures look to be in \nthe 50s mostly on Thursday as daytime heating somewhat counters \nthe cooler airmass. Friday looks to be similar day to Thursday \nas the high pressure moves out. The best rain chances at the \nmoment look to stay to our south.\n\nThis weekend into early next week... A larger scale system looks\nto be moving across the CONUS this weekend. Deterministic storm\ntracks now favor areas to our south and this is reflected in the\nensemble QPF trends as well. Won't say that we will be dry, but\nthe trend has been moving lower. Still could see especially\nsouthern Minnesota seeing some rain from this. It is more so\ngoing into early next week on Monday/Tuesday that is better \nchance right now. All deterministic global models take a deep \nsurface low right across the north central CONUS. There remains \na lot of spread to determine what sector we'd be in and what the\nimpacts will be, but this is the one to keep an eye on for this\nperiod. Saturday temperature wise looks to be more 50s. Sunday \ncould start to be warmer with WAA ahead of this next system \nbuoying us into the 60s.",
